{{#lazyNativeImport}}
### Native lazy imports

This client was generated with `lazyNativeImport=true` and requires Python 3.15+
and Pydantic 2.14+. Its generated API and model imports use `lazy from`: modules
load on first use, so applications that use only a subset of a large client's
Pydantic models can avoid loading unused modules and reduce startup work and
initial memory use.

Import costs move to first use; import failures and module initialization side
effects can happen later. Evaluating type annotations, constructing schemas, or
using models can resolve lazy imports. Gains depend on the workload, and native
imports are not guaranteed to use less memory than `lazyImports=true`.
The two import options cannot be enabled together. Combining native imports with
`deferBuild=true` can also reduce initial memory use.
{{/lazyNativeImport}}
{{#deferBuild}}
### Deferred operation validators

This client was generated with `deferBuild=true`. Its `@validate_call` decorators
set `defer_build=True`, building each operation's argument validator on its first
call. This can reduce startup work and memory for operations that are never
called. Argument validation remains enabled; construction costs and schema
construction errors move to first call.

This setting does not change `model_config`. In the evaluated POC, also deferring
models increased retained memory. The diagnostic evidence pointed to reduced
reuse of nested validators and serializers, plus pending-model placeholders.
This is workload- and Pydantic-version-dependent, not a universal limitation.
`deferBuild` can be used on its own or with either lazy import strategy.
{{/deferBuild}}
